WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.979 --> 00:00:03.179
Posit Cloud is an online platform where

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:04.920
you can easily do and share data work

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.440
with just a few clicks. We recently added

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.300
a new feature called Data Connections

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.340
which lets you securely store database

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.320
credentials and share them across data

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.020
projects and among your collaborators.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.180
In this tutorial, we will demonstrate how

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.920
to achieve this by using a publicly

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.740
available Postgres database. Let's get

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.180
started.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.920
The first thing we're going to do is

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.199
create a new space. You can find this in

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:30.539
the upper left-hand nav bar.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:32.160
Let's go ahead and create a new space,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.559
calling it RNA Project.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.079
This will create a new space for us to

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:42.180
do work and invite others to collaborate.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.280
You can start a new project, but before

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.860
we do that, let's go to Data.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.300
This is where you're going to add your

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.399
new data connection.

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:53.480
Click on add connection

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.079
and we're going to make a new connection

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:56.160
name.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.559
Let's type in RNAcentral,

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:01.500
which is going to be a Postgres database.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.180
These credentials are publicly available

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.939
and linked to in the description.

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:09.000
Our user is going to be reader.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.100
I'll paste in the password, which you can

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.720
find online,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.040
and the server,

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.360
finally, we're going to go ahead and put

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.000
in our port number

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.380
and our database name.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.820
Once we do this, we're going to click on

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.920
OK,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.900
the database connection is going to be

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.860
stored and saved

00:01:34.619 --> 00:01:36.000
and we're going to be able to use this

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.939
in projects, so let's go back to content

00:01:38.939 --> 00:01:41.759
and now let's create a new

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:46.280
RStudio IDE project from the drop-down.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.479
While the project is loading, let's

00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:52.759
rename it

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:56.759
RNA Analysis.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.720
Once Posit Cloud is done spinning up

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.840
your individualized IDE,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.579
we're going to be able to access that

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:09.420
database connection that we just defined

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.459
so if you look in the upper right-hand

00:02:11.459 --> 00:02:13.319
of the IDE,

00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:16.260
and move to the connections tab,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.959
we're going to start a new connection

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:21.000
and there it is: the database connection

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.120
we just saved, RNAcentral.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.760
Clicking on this is going to show you a

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.379
code snippet, which includes all of the

00:02:28.379 --> 00:02:30.780
information, including the encrypted

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.760
password.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.340
Let's use a new R script

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.319
to connect to

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:39.720
and analyze the data.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.760
The first thing this is going to do is

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.800
load in a necessary package, and while

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.980
it's doing that let's name the R script

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.660
rna_explorer.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.420
From here, we can then load the library

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:55.980
that we need

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.420
and the DBI library, and create our first

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.720
connection.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.340
The connection has been made, and you can

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.560
see in the connections tab all of the

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.319
schemas available for this database

00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:12.620
connection.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.379
Before we make our first query, let's

00:03:16.379 --> 00:03:18.480
bring in two libraries:

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.780
dplyr and dbplyr.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.519
Now, neither of these have been installed

00:03:23.519 --> 00:03:24.659
yet

00:03:24.659 --> 00:03:27.000
but the IDE will remind me of this and

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.500
we can install right away.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.860
Once these packages are ready, we're

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.280
going to go ahead and make a query

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.519
and we can do this by using the con

00:03:41.519 --> 00:03:43.739
variable that has stored the connection

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:47.180
that is still live.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.920
Let's go ahead and create a new variable

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.440
named results.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.760
From here,

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:58.080
we're going to call the connection

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.480
and then bring in the specific schema

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.000
name and table that we're interested in.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:07.379
For now,

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:09.480
we're just going to go ahead and get the

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.700
top 200 results within this table.

00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:18.660
When I run this,

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.060
we went to the database, we got all the

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.979
information on the top 200 rows, and now

00:04:22.979 --> 00:04:25.440
we can see this in the environment. We

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.120
have both our connection still

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.460
and results data frame, which has 200

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.080
observations.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:32.940
If we take a quick peek,

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.680
we can see the type of information that

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.240
is stored

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:37.860
and there seems to be an integer

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.020
variable length

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.259
for each of the observations.

00:04:43.259 --> 00:04:44.880
We can quickly turn this into a

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:45.960
histogram

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:49.400
to see what we're working with.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.759
Some observations are in the 0 to 500

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:55.259
range

00:04:55.259 --> 00:05:00.720
while many are up around 1200 or above.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.300
The best part is if you create a new

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.040
project, you'll be able to access the

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:07.500
same database connection,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.840
and if you change the information from

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.820
your connection, it'll automatically

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.120
update in new and existing projects once

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.440
it's relaunched.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.479
I hope you enjoyed learning about the

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:20.220
new data connections feature in Posit

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:21.720
Cloud.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.479
You can visit posit.cloud to create a

00:05:24.479 --> 00:05:26.639
free account, connect to your databases,

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:30.080
and begin exploring.

